  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":28,"t":"single_choice","p":"The figures show arrays of dots. Figure 1 has 2 dots, figure 2 has 6 dots and figure 3 has 12 dots. In figure n the array has n rows and n + 1 columns. Which rule gives the number of dots Dₙ in figure n?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The figures show arrays of dots. Figure 1 has 2 dots, figure 2 has 6 dots and figure 3 has 12 dots. In figure n the array has n rows and n + 1 columns. Which rule gives the number of dots Dₙ in figure n?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp5/q28_dots.jpg","alt":"Arrays of dots for figures 1, 2 and 3 (2, 6 and 12 dots)","width":96}}]},"e":"n rows × (n + 1) columns = n(n + 1) = n² + n dots. Check: n = 1 → 2 ✓; n = 2 → 6 ✓; n = 3 → 12 ✓.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":28,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Rows × columns.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Dₙ = n(n + 1) ÷ 2","Dₙ = n² + 1","Dₙ = 2n²","Dₙ = n² + n"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["The array is n by n + 1.","Multiply the row and column counts, then expand."],"figure":"myp5_q28_dots"},"marking":""},{"n":29,"t":"single_choice","p":"The area of a pond covered by algae is measured every week: 5 m², 8 m², 13 m², 20 m². The measurements form a quadratic sequence. What is the constant second difference?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The area of a pond covered by algae is measured every week: 5 m², 8 m², 13 m², 20 m². The measurements form a quadratic sequence. What is the constant second difference?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"measurement"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"week 1"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"week 2"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"week 3"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"week 4"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"algae area (m²)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"13"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"first differences"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"second differences"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]}]}]},"e":"First differences: 8 − 5 = 3, 13 − 8 = 5, 20 − 13 = 7. Second differences: 5 − 3 = 2, 7 − 5 = 2 — constant 2.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":29,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Differences of the differences.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["1","3","2","5"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["First differences: 3, 5, 7.","The gaps of the gaps are identical — what constant value is it?"]},"marking":""},{"n":30,"t":"single_choice","p":"Four sequences are described by what happens between their terms. Which one is quadratic?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four sequences are described by what happens between their terms. Which one is quadratic?"}]}]},"e":"Quadratic ⇔ constant non-zero second differences ⇔ first differences that change by a constant amount. 3, 5, 7, 9 changes by 2 each time. 4, 4, 4, 4 is linear; 1, 2, 4, 8 doubles (like a geometric pattern); constant ratio 2 is geometric.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":30,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Look for first differences that grow by a constant amount.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Its first differences are 4, 4, 4, 4","Its first differences are 1, 2, 4, 8","The ratio between consecutive terms is always 2","Its first differences are 3, 5, 7, 9"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Constant first differences → linear.","Check whether the gaps of the gaps are constant and non-zero."]},"marking":""},{"n":31,"t":"single_choice","p":"Find the nth term of the quadratic sequence 3, 9, 17, 27, 39, …","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Find the nth term of the quadratic sequence 3, 9, 17, 27, 39, …"}]}]},"e":"Differences: 6, 8, 10, 12; second difference 2 ⇒ coefficient of n² is 1. Compare with n²: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25; subtracting: 2, 5, 8, 11, 14 = 3n − 1. So uₙ = n² + 3n − 1 (check n = 1: 1 + 3 − 1 = 3 ✓; n = 4: 16 + 12 − 1 = 27 ✓).\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":31,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Use the second difference to fix the n² coefficient.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["n² + 6n − 1","n² + 3n + 3","n² + 3n − 1","2n² + 3n − 1"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Second difference is 2, so start from n².","Fix the coefficients of n and the constant by comparison with n²."]},"marking":""},{"n":32,"t":"single_choice","p":"A ball is dropped from a tower. Its height above the ground, in metres, is modelled by h = 100 − 5t², where t is the time in seconds. The heights recorded at each whole second form a quadratic sequence. At which recorded time is the ball first below 30 m?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A ball is dropped from a tower. Its height above the ground, in metres, is modelled by h = 100 − 5t², where t is the time in seconds. The heights recorded at each whole second form a quadratic sequence. At which recorded time is the ball first below 30 m?"}]}]},"e":"t = 2: h = 100 − 20 = 80 m; t = 3: h = 100 − 45 = 55 m; t = 4: h = 100 − 80 = 20 m < 30 m; t = 5: h = 25 m. The first recorded time with h < 30 is t = 4 s.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":32,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Evaluate h at successive whole seconds.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["t = 2 s","t = 4 s","t = 5 s","t = 3 s"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Evaluate h at t = 1, 2, 3, 4 and find the first time below 30 m.","The answer is the first whole second that satisfies the condition."]},"marking":""},{"n":33,"t":"single_choice","p":"At a market meeting, every trader shakes hands exactly once with every other trader. With 2, 3, 4 and 5 traders there are 1, 3, 6 and 10 handshakes — a quadratic sequence. A student says the rule is Hₙ = n² − n. Which statement is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At a market meeting, every trader shakes hands exactly once with every other trader. With 2, 3, 4 and 5 traders there are 1, 3, 6 and 10 handshakes — a quadratic sequence. A student says the rule is Hₙ = n² − n. Which statement is correct?"}]}]},"e":"Handshakes = pairs from n traders = n(n − 1) ÷ 2: 2 → 1, 3 → 3, 4 → 6, 5 → 10 ✓. The rule n² − n gives 2, 6, 12, 20 — exactly double, because each pair is counted twice (once for each of its two members).\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":33,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Each pair is counted twice in n(n − 1).","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The correct rule is Hₙ = n(n + 1) ÷ 2, because the pattern always grows to the next number","The correct rule is Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2, because each of the n traders shakes hands with n − 1 others and every pair is counted twice","The rule n² − n is correct because the second differences are 2","The correct rule is Hₙ = n², because each trader meets n others"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["n traders each shake n − 1 hands — that’s n(n − 1), but every handshake appears twice.","Halve the product — each handshake was counted once per trader."]},"marking":""},{"n":34,"t":"single_choice","p":"A short film is shared in a neighbourhood. It gets 5 views on day 1, 20 views on day 2, 80 views on day 3 and 320 views on day 4. The daily totals form a geometric sequence. What is the common ratio?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A short film is shared in a neighbourhood. It gets 5 views on day 1, 20 views on day 2, 80 views on day 3 and 320 views on day 4. The daily totals form a geometric sequence. What is the common ratio?"}]}]},"e":"20 ÷ 5 = 4; 80 ÷ 20 = 4; 320 ÷ 80 = 4. The common ratio is 4.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":34,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Divide consecutive terms.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["4","5","16","15"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Three multiplies take the first term to the fourth — what is the repeated factor?","Every term is 4 times the previous one."]},"marking":""},{"n":35,"t":"single_choice","p":"A geometric sequence has first term 3 and common ratio 2. What is the 6th term?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence has first term 3 and common ratio 2. What is the 6th term?"}]}]},"e":"u₆ = 3 × 2⁵ = 3 × 32 = 96.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":35,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Fifth power of 2, times 3.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["96","64","48","192"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["uₙ = 3 × 2ⁿ⁻¹ — apply n = 6.","Apply n = 6 to the rule — three times a power of 2."]},"marking":""},{"n":36,"t":"single_choice","p":"Four situations are described by their first four values. Which one is a geometric sequence?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four situations are described by their first four values. Which one is a geometric sequence?"}]}]},"e":"Ratios: A) +4 (linear) ✗; B) 4, 2.25, 1.78 — not constant (quadratic, n²·2) ✗; C) not constant ✗; D) 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 — constant ratio 0.5 ✓ geometric (halving each bounce).\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":36,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Compute the ratio of consecutive terms.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Distance run each day, in km: 4, 8, 12, 16","Areas of square plots, in m²: 2, 8, 18, 32","Price of a bag of rice over four weeks, in FCFA: 5 000, 5 000, 6 000, 6 000","Heights of a bouncing ball, in cm: 100, 50, 25, 12.5"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Geometric means ‘multiply by the same number each step’.","Test which candidate list is multiplied by the same factor each step."]},"marking":""},{"n":37,"t":"single_choice","p":"A student writes the explicit rule of the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, … as aₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ. What is wrong with this rule?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student writes the explicit rule of the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, … as aₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ. What is wrong with this rule?"}]}]},"e":"n = 1: 2 × 3¹ = 6 ≠ 2. The first term of the rule is 6, not 2 — the exponent should be n − 1: aₙ = 2 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ (check: n = 1 → 2 ✓, n = 2 → 6 ✓, n = 3 → 18 ✓).\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":37,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Test the student’s rule at n = 1.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The rule gives 6 as the first term; the exponent should be n − 1 so that a₁ = 2","The rule should be aₙ = 3 × 2ⁿ, because the first term is 2","The rule should divide by 3 instead of multiplying by 3","Nothing is wrong: the rule gives the terms of the sequence"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["n = 1 gives 2 × 3 = 6, but the first term is 2.","Shift the exponent down by one and recheck against the printed first term."]},"marking":""},{"n":38,"t":"single_choice","p":"A culture of bacteria doubles every hour, starting from 250 bacteria at hour 0. How many bacteria are there after 5 hours?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A culture of bacteria doubles every hour, starting from 250 bacteria at hour 0. How many bacteria are there after 5 hours?"}]}]},"e":"B₅ = 250 × 2⁵ = 250 × 32 = 8000 bacteria.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":38,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Five doublings: 250 × 2⁵.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["4 000","8 000","1 500","16 000"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["After n hours the count is 250 × 2ⁿ.","Apply n = 5 to the rule — 250 times a power of 2."]},"marking":""},{"n":39,"t":"single_choice","p":"A sheet of paper is folded in half and the number of layers is recorded after each fold: 2, 4, 8, 16. Which rule gives the number of layers Lₙ after n folds?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sheet of paper is folded in half and the number of layers is recorded after each fold: 2, 4, 8, 16. Which rule gives the number of layers Lₙ after n folds?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"number of folds n"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lₙ (layers)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"16"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Each fold doubles the layers, and after 1 fold there are 2 layers: Lₙ = 2 × 2ⁿ⁻¹ = 2ⁿ. Check: n = 1 → 2 ✓; n = 4 → 16 ✓.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":39,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Double each time: powers of 2.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Lₙ = 2ⁿ⁻¹","Lₙ = 2n","Lₙ = 2ⁿ","Lₙ = n²"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["2, 4, 8, 16 — these are powers of 2.","After n folds, the exponent matches the fold count."]},"marking":""},{"n":40,"t":"single_choice","p":"A motorbike is bought for 800 000 FCFA. Every year it loses 15% of its value. What is its value at the end of the 3rd year?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A motorbike is bought for 800 000 FCFA. Every year it loses 15% of its value. What is its value at the end of the 3rd year?"}]}]},"e":"Each year the bike keeps 85% of its value: V₃ = 800 000 × 0.85³ = 800 000 × 0.614 125 = 491 300 FCFA.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":40,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Multiply by 0.85 three times.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["578 000 FCFA","440 000 FCFA","417 605 FCFA","491 300 FCFA"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Losing 15% means keeping 85% each year.","Apply the 85% retention three times in a row, then evaluate."]},"marking":""},{"n":41,"t":"single_choice","p":"The geometric sequence 6, 18, 54, … contains one term equal to 13 122. Which term number is it?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The geometric sequence 6, 18, 54, … contains one term equal to 13 122. Which term number is it?"}]}]},"e":"uₙ = 6 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 13 122 → 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 2187 = 3⁷ → n − 1 = 7 → n = 8.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":41,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Divide 13 122 by 6, then find the power of 3.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["the 8th term","the 9th term","the 6th term","the 7th term"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Divide the term by the first term to isolate the power.","Write that quotient as a power of 3 and read off the exponent; add 1."]},"marking":""},{"n":42,"t":"single_choice","p":"In 2020, two neighbouring villages each have 12 000 people. Village A gains 500 people every year, while village B grows by 3% every year. Which statement correctly compares their populations in 2030?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In 2020, two neighbouring villages each have 12 000 people. Village A gains 500 people every year, while village B grows by 3% every year. Which statement correctly compares their populations in 2030?"}]}]},"e":"A: 12 000 + 10 × 500 = 17 000. B: 12 000 × 1.03¹⁰ ≈ 12 000 × 1.3439 ≈ 16 127. A is larger in 2030, but B’s proportional growth eventually overtakes A (B% of a growing base grows too).\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":42,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Compute both 2030 populations.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Village B, because a percentage growth always gives more than a fixed growth","Village B, because 3% of 12 000 is 500 people","The two villages have the same population, because they both started from 12 000 people","Village A, with 17 000 people against about 16 127 for village B — but B grows proportionally, so it overtakes A later"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Project village A to 2030 with straight-line growth.","Project village B to 2030 with 3% compound growth and compare; also think further ahead."]},"marking":""},{"n":43,"t":"single_choice","p":"Kadi reads 4 pages on the first day, and on each following day she reads 3 more pages than the day before. How many pages does she read in total during the first 10 days?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kadi reads 4 pages on the first day, and on each following day she reads 3 more pages than the day before. How many pages does she read in total during the first 10 days?"}]}]},"e":"S₁₀ = 10 ÷ 2 × (2 × 4 + 9 × 3) = 5 × (8 + 27) = 5 × 35 = 175 pages.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":43,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Use Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × [2a + (n − 1)d].","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["175","310","190","350"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["a = 4, d = 3, n = 10.","Substitute a, d and n into the sum formula and simplify."]},"marking":""},{"n":44,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the sum of the first 20 terms of the arithmetic sequence with first term 5 and common difference 4?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the sum of the first 20 terms of the arithmetic sequence with first term 5 and common difference 4?"}]}]},"e":"S₂₀ = 20 ÷ 2 × (2 × 5 + 19 × 4) = 10 × (10 + 76) = 10 × 86 = 860.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":44,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Substitute into the sum formula.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["860","900","810","1 620"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["a = 5, d = 4, n = 20.","Bracket first, then multiply by n ÷ 2."]},"marking":""},{"n":45,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which formula gives the sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which formula gives the sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series?"}]}]},"e":"The arithmetic series sum formula is Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × [2a₁ + (n − 1)d] (equivalently n ÷ 2 × (a₁ + aₙ)). Option A is the nth-term formula; option D forgets the division by 2.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":45,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Distinguish the nth-term formula from the sum formula.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Sₙ = a₁ + (n − 1)d","Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × [2a₁ + (n − 1)d]","Sₙ = n ÷ 2 × [a₁ + (n − 1)d]","Sₙ = n × (a₁ + aₙ)"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Option A is the formula for the nth term, not the sum.","It averages the end terms and multiplies by the count."]},"marking":""},{"n":46,"t":"single_choice","p":"In a tontine (a rotating savings group), 12 members pay their contribution one after another. The first member pays 12 000 FCFA and each following member pays 800 FCFA more than the member before. What is the total amount collected?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a tontine (a rotating savings group), 12 members pay their contribution one after another. The first member pays 12 000 FCFA and each following member pays 800 FCFA more than the member before. What is the total amount collected?"}]}]},"e":"S₁₂ = 12 ÷ 2 × (2 × 12 000 + 11 × 800) = 6 × (24 000 + 8 800) = 6 × 32 800 = 196 800 FCFA.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":46,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"a = 12 000, d = 800, n = 12.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["196 800 FCFA","144 000 FCFA","393 600 FCFA","201 600 FCFA"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["S₁₂ = 6 × (24 000 + 8 800).","Evaluate the bracket, then multiply by 6."]},"marking":""},{"n":47,"t":"single_choice","p":"Adding the first odd numbers gives interesting totals: 1, 1 + 3, 1 + 3 + 5 and 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 equal 1, 4, 9 and 16. Which conjecture about the sum of the first n odd numbers is correct, and what is the sum of the first 25 odd numbers?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Adding the first odd numbers gives interesting totals: 1, 1 + 3, 1 + 3 + 5 and 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 equal 1, 4, 9 and 16. Which conjecture about the sum of the first n odd numbers is correct, and what is the sum of the first 25 odd numbers?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"odd numbers added"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total written as a square"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1²"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 + 3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2²"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 + 3 + 5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3²"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 + 3 + 5 + 7"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"16"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4²"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 + 3 + … + 49"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]}]}]},"e":"1 = 1², 1 + 3 = 4 = 2², 1 + 3 + 5 = 9 = 3², 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 16 = 4² — the sum of the first n odd numbers is n². For n = 25: 625.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":47,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Spot the square pattern.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The sum is n(n + 1), so the total is 650","The sum is n², so the first 25 odd numbers add up to 625","The sum is n(n + 1) ÷ 2, so the total is 325","The sum is 2n − 1, so the total is 49"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Each total is a perfect square.","25² = 625."]},"marking":""},{"n":48,"t":"single_choice","p":"Find the sum 7 + 11 + 15 + … + 59.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Find the sum 7 + 11 + 15 + … + 59."}]}]},"e":"a₁ = 7, d = 4; n = (59 − 7) ÷ 4 + 1 = 14 terms. S = 14 ÷ 2 × (7 + 59) = 7 × 66 = 462.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":48,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Count the terms first.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["495","429","462","924"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Count the terms: from the first to the last in steps of d.","Multiply the term count by the average of the first and last terms."]},"marking":""},{"n":49,"t":"single_choice","p":"A delivery driver drives 45 km on Monday. From Tuesday onwards she drives 6 km less than the day before, and she works from Monday to Sunday. How many kilometres does she drive during that week?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A delivery driver drives 45 km on Monday. From Tuesday onwards she drives 6 km less than the day before, and she works from Monday to Sunday. How many kilometres does she drive during that week?"}]}]},"e":"Distances: 45, 39, 33, 27, 21, 15, 9. S₇ = 7 ÷ 2 × (2 × 45 + 6 × (−6)) = 3.5 × (90 − 36) = 3.5 × 54 = 189 km.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":49,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"a = 45, d = −6, n = 7.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["180 km","189 km","168 km","378 km"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["n ÷ 2 = 3.5 for seven terms; the end terms are in the question.","Evaluate the bracket, then multiply by 3.5."]},"marking":""},{"n":50,"t":"single_choice","p":"The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series is given by Sₙ = 3n² + 5n. What are the first term and the common difference of this series?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series is given by Sₙ = 3n² + 5n. What are the first term and the common difference of this series?"}]}]},"e":"a₁ = S₁ = 3 + 5 = 8. uₙ = Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁ = (3n² + 5n) − (3(n−1)² + 5(n−1)) = 6n + 2. So the terms are 8, 14, 20, … — the common difference is 6.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Series","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":50,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"a₁ = S₁; difference via Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["a₁ = 8, d = 3","a₁ = 5, d = 3","a₁ = 8, d = 6","a₁ = 3, d = 10"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["S₁ = 8, so a₁ = 8.","The term is the difference of consecutive partial sums — expand it."]},"marking":""}]'::jsonb) as x
